==================================================================
DAY 10 - PART A: THEORY
ReplicaSets, Labels, and Selectors
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. THE PROBLEM
--------------
A bare pod dies and stays dead. We want "always keep 3 copies
running". That is the job of a REPLICASET.


2. LABELS
---------
Labels are key/value tags attached to any object:
  metadata:
    labels:
      app: web
      env: prod
      tier: frontend

They mean nothing to Kubernetes by themselves. They become powerful
because other objects SELECT by them. Labels are how Kubernetes glues
things together (ReplicaSet -> pods, Service -> pods, NetworkPolicy ->
pods, nodeSelector -> nodes).

Annotations are also key/value but are NOT used for selection; they
store extra info (build ID, owner, tool config).


3. SELECTORS
------------
Equality-based:   app=web       env!=prod
Set-based:        env in (dev,test)    tier notin (db)    !legacy

  k get pods -l app=web
  k get pods -l 'env in (dev,test)'
  k get pods -L app,env      (show labels as columns)


4. REPLICASET
-------------
A ReplicaSet has three key parts:

  apiVersion: apps/v1
  kind: ReplicaSet
  metadata:
    name: web-rs
  spec:
    replicas: 3              <- how many
    selector:                <- which pods count as "mine"
      matchLabels:
        app: web
    template:                <- how to create a new pod
      metadata:
        labels:
          app: web           <- MUST match the selector
      spec:
        containers:
        - name: web
          image: nginx:1.27

The ReplicaSet controller loop:
  count pods matching selector  ->  compare with replicas  ->
  create from template or delete extras.


5. IMPORTANT BEHAVIOURS
-----------------------
- The ReplicaSet owns its pods (see metadata.ownerReferences on a pod).
- If you change a pod's label so it no longer matches, the ReplicaSet
  "lets it go" (orphans it) and creates a replacement.
- If you create a bare pod WITH matching labels, the ReplicaSet may
  adopt it and delete one to stay at the desired count.
- Changing the template (e.g. image) does NOT update existing pods.
  Only NEW pods get the new template. That is why we use Deployments
  for updates (Day 11).


6. WHERE YOU SEE REPLICASETS IN REAL LIFE
-----------------------------------------
You almost never create ReplicaSets directly. Deployments create and
manage them for you. But understanding them explains how Deployments
do rolling updates.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Labels tag, selectors pick. They connect everything in Kubernetes.
- ReplicaSet = replicas + selector + template.
- Template labels must match the selector.
- ReplicaSets don't update existing pods; Deployments do.
